  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Lasioglossum marinum is a small sweat bee species belonging to the family Halictidae, native to coastal and near-coastal regions of New England. These solitary to semi-social bees are important pollinators of native plants and are attracted to human perspiration, earning the common name 'sweat bees.'",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Size and Color",
      "description": "Very small bee, typically 4-6mm in length, with a dark metallic green to black body",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Facial Features",
      "description": "Distinctive large compound eyes and relatively short antennae compared to other bee species",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Coastal Preference",
      "description": "More commonly found in maritime environments and salt marsh areas compared to inland Lasioglossum species",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Lasioglossum marinum prefers coastal habitats, salt marshes, and areas near beaches in New England, where it forages on salt-tolerant native flowering plants.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite their tiny size, sweat bees like L. marinum have a keen sense of smell and are attracted to the salt and amino acids in human sweat, sometimes landing on people during outdoor activities.",
  "confusionNotes": null,
